  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 108 PM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nWest-northwest flow aloft will advect a warmer airmass into the \nregion tomorrow, with the High Resolution Ensemble Forecast (HREF) \nsystem mean bringing 850mb temperatures into the 17-18 degC range. \nForecast soundings suggest that during the afternoon we'll mix into \nthis warm layer to bring surface temperatures into the mid to upper \n80s. Meanwhile, a decaying cool front will approach the area from \nthe north tomorrow night into Thursday, and as high pressure settles \nover Iowa the local area will see a return to northeast surface \nwinds which may keep it slightly cooler Thursday afternoon - \nparticularly north of Highway 60. Friday, however, the unseasonable \nwarmth will surge back northward to encompass the entire area as \nridging strengthens over the Mississippi River Valley and deep \nsouthwest flow consequently overtakes the local area. Given the \ncontinued dry weather, drought conditions will worsen throughout the \nweek.\n\nUncertainty increases in the weather over the weekend as models \nstruggle to handle phasing between a cutoff low lifting north out of \nArklatex and a tropical system currently evolving over the southwest \nGulf. Spaghetti plots from various hurricane models suggest invest \n92L, which the NHC gives nearly 100% chance of developing into a \nTropical Cyclone (TC) over the next 48 hours, will move north across \nthe Lower-Mid Mississippi Valley before getting lifted northeastward \nacross the Ohio Valley Sunday into Monday. Given most guidance keeps \nthe track to our east, it remains unclear whether we'll see (and if \nso, then how much) rainfall from this feature, but probabilities of \nmeasurable rain from the National Blend of Models (NBM) range from \nless than 5% across far western Missouri to 40% across areas east of \nroughly Highway 63.",
